      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.602,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.058
          ],
          "content": "Atomic Foundations of Matter"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "179 N"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.087,
            0.775,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": "Ionic compounds generally have high melting and boiling points due to strong inter-ionic attractions, whereas covalent compounds usually have low melting and boiling points."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.14,
            0.161,
            0.171
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.144,
            0.353,
            0.164
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Pause and Ponder"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.172,
            0.813,
            0.205
          ],
          "content": "19. What type of chemical bond is present in a solid compound that does not conduct electricity in the solid state but conducts electricity when dissolved in water?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.206,
            0.812,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": "20. Metal M, with two electrons in its valence shell (M shell), reacts with oxygen to form a compound that is slightly soluble in water. Predict its:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.239,
            0.27,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": "(i) formula"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.257,
            0.305,
            0.273
          ],
          "content": "(ii) type of bond"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.274,
            0.57,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "(iii) electrical conductivity of its aqueous solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.323,
            0.671,
            0.345
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "9.7 Molecular Mass of Covalent Compounds"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.348,
            0.777,
            0.4
          ],
          "content": "Since you know the formulae of the covalent compounds, you can find the masses of their molecules by simply adding up the masses of the atoms present in them."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.785,
            0.311,
            0.952,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.405,
            0.242,
            0.423
          ],
          "content": "**Example 9.4**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.426,
            0.387,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": "Molecular mass of water $( \\mathrm { H } _ { 2 } \\mathrm { O } )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.448,
            0.402,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": "Atomic mass — H = 1 u; O = 16 u"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.82,
            0.431,
            0.914,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": "(a) Camphor"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.467,
            0.566,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": "Molecular mass of $\\mathrm{H_2O} = (1\\mathrm{~u} \\times 2) + (16\\mathrm{~u} \\times 1) = 18\\mathrm{~u}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.492,
            0.242,
            0.51
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Example 9.5"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.513,
            0.465,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": "Molecular mass of carbon dioxide (CO )"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.534,
            0.409,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": "Atomic mass — C = 12 u; O = 16 u"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.554,
            0.575,
            0.572
          ],
          "content": "Molecular Mass of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}=(12\\mathrm{~u}\\times1)+(16\\mathrm{~u}\\times2)=44\\mathrm{~u}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.575,
            0.773,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": "You have learnt in section 9.4.2 that in ionic compounds, the ions form 3-D crystals, i.e., ionic compounds do not form molecules."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.784,
            0.457,
            0.95,
            0.572
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.799,
                0.577,
                0.933,
                0.625
              ],
              "content": "(b) Naphthalene Fig. 9.17: Covalent compounds"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_23.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.619,
            0.185,
            0.647
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_image_25.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.191,
            0.624,
            0.382,
            0.643
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Pause and Ponder"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.652,
            0.542,
            0.686
          ],
          "content": "21. Find the molecular mass of nitric acid (HNO ). Atomic mass — H = 1 u; $N = 14   u ; \\; O = 16   u .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.689,
            0.517,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": "22. Find the molecular mass of methane (CH ). Atomic mass $C = 12   u ;  H = 1   u .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.75,
            0.662,
            0.772
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "9.8 Formula Unit Mass of Ionic Compounds"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.774,
            0.775,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": "In ionic compounds, the collection of the simplest whole number ratio of ions is termed as a **formula unit**. The mass of a formula unit is called the **formula unit mass**."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.832,
            0.242,
            0.85
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Example 9.6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.853,
            0.49,
            0.871
          ],
          "content": "Formula unit mass of sodium oxide $( \\mathrm { N a } _ { 2 } \\mathrm { O } )$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.874,
            0.419,
            0.89
          ],
          "content": "Atomic mass — Na = 23 u; O = 16 u"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.894,
            0.621,
            0.912
          ],
          "content": "Formula unit mass of $\\mathrm{Na}_{2}\\mathrm{O}=(23\\mathrm{~u}\\times2)+(16\\mathrm{~u}\\times1)=62\\mathrm{~u}$"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
